narrative:
## Variables as Story Blanks

In Mad Libs, you collect random words and insert them into a pre-written story. In programming, you use variables as the blanks. The story stays the same, but every time you change the variables, you get a new, ridiculous tale.

### Building a Mad Lib

Let's collect a name, an adjective, a noun, and a verb, then weave them into a story:

```xiom
fn main() {
  let silly_name = "Captain Waffles";
  let adjective = "sparkly";
  let noun = "toaster";
  let verb = "dance";

  io.println("Once upon a time, there was a hero named:");
  io.println(silly_name);
  io.println("");

  let line1 = string.str_concat(silly_name, " found a ");
  let line2 = string.str_concat(line1, adjective);
  let line3 = string.str_concat(line2, " ");
  let line4 = string.str_concat(line3, noun);
  io.println(line4);

  let line5 = string.str_concat("The ", noun);
  let line6 = string.str_concat(line5, " started to ");
  let line7 = string.str_concat(line6, verb);
  let line8 = string.str_concat(line7, " all by itself!");
  io.println(line8);

  io.println("Everyone laughed and cheered. The end!");
}
```

This prints a silly story about Captain Waffles finding a sparkly toaster that started to dance!

### How It Works

Each variable holds a word. `string.str_concat` glues the pieces together around the variable words. The story structure is fixed, but the words are flexible. Change `silly_name` to "Princess Pickles" and `noun` to "refrigerator" — suddenly it's a completely different tale!

### Why This Is Powerful

This is how real apps create personalized content. When a game says "Welcome back, [your username]!", it's using the same pattern: a template with a variable slot where your name gets inserted.

